    "result_text": "The change is complete and the suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country E.164 national-number length validation in the owner-create pipeline.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java` (the `normalizeTelephone` step of `POST /api/owners`):\n- Added a `COUNTRY_RULES` table mapping country codes to their required national-digit counts (`+61` \u2192 9, `+1` \u2192 10), ordered longest-code-first so `61` is matched before `1`.\n- After E.164 assembly, when the caller supplied an **explicit** country code, the national part is now held to that country's exact length; a wrong length returns `null`, which the existing `reject(...)` turns into a **400** (`MethodArgumentNotValidException` on field `telephone`) \u2014 the same shape as the other telephone validation failures.\n- Updated the Javadoc and the rejection message to state the new rule.\n\n**Why scoped to explicit country codes:** I checked the actual tests before deciding.\n- The **new** acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` posts `+61 123` (explicit `+61`, wrong national length) expecting 400, and its valid fixture `04XXXXXXXX` deliberately yields 9 national digits under `+61`.\n- The **existing** v1 controller tests post 10-digit US numbers with *no* `+`, relying on the default-`+61` assumption, and expect success (e.g. `(608) 555-0002` \u2192 `+616085550002`).\n\nEnforcing the strict length on the *default-assumed* path would have broken those existing tests; the spec's `+61`/`+1` examples are themselves explicit-country-code forms. This scoping both fully enforces the rule where a country is actually declared (e.g. `+611234567890` and `+1202555012` are now correctly rejected, which the previous loose 8\u201315 bound wrongly accepted) and keeps every existing test valid.\n\n**Verification:** full `./mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, 261 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, 0 skipped (including the new `AcceptanceTest` and all 28 `OwnerRestControllerV1Tests`).\n\nOne note for your awareness: enforcement is applied only when the request includes an explicit `+` country code. A number sent *without* a country code that normalizes to a wrong-length `+61` value (e.g. bare `6085550002` \u2192 `+616085550002`, 10 national digits) is still accepted \u2014 this is required to keep the existing v1 create tests passing. If you'd rather enforce the length on the default-assumed path too, that's a deliberate follow-up that would require updating those existing test fixtures.",
